# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3 have "borrowed_terms → controversies"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 has "borrowed_terms → controversies" and "borrowed_terms"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2, to: Suppose "men are used to speak of the nature and operations of the mind in terms borrowed from sensible ideas" and "men are engaged in controversies and mistakes with regard to the nature and operations of the mind" are false. Is S1, "But, nothing seems more to have contributed towards engaging men in controversies and mistakes with regard to the nature and operations of the mind, than the being used to speak of those things in terms borrowed from sensible ideas.", true then?
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1 has nothing from it; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3 have "ball_belief → moral_errors"; they differ when only "there is a belief that the mind of man is as a ball in motion, impelled and determined by the objects of sense as necessarily as a ball by the stroke of a racket" is true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "there is a belief that the mind of man is as a ball in motion, impelled and determined by the objects of sense as necessarily as a ball by the stroke of a racket" is true and "endless scruples and errors of dangerous consequence arise in morality" is false. Is S3, "Hence arise endless scruples and errors of dangerous consequence in morality.", true then?
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 3 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1 has "reflect → cleared"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 has "reflect → ◇cleared"; they differ when only "philosophers retire into themselves and attentively consider their own meaning" and "possibly, these errors are cleared, and truth appears plain, uniform and consistent" are true, which the second allows; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S4 is read berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3 with no modality; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 as what is necessary or possible: its "may" or "could" does not say which it means
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered yes, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "philosophers retire into themselves and attentively consider their own meaning" and "possibly, these errors are cleared, and truth appears plain, uniform and consistent" are true and "these errors are cleared, and truth appears plain, uniform and consistent" is false. Is S4, "All which, I doubt not, may be cleared, and truth appear plain, uniform, and consistent, could but philosophers be prevailed on to retire into themselves, and attentively consider their own meaning.", true then?
berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1 has "ball_belief → moral_errors"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3 have nothing from it; they differ when only "there is a belief that the mind of man is as a ball in motion, impelled and determined by the objects of sense as necessarily as a ball by the stroke of a racket" is true, which the second allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1 has "moral_errors"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3 have "reflect → ◇cleared"; they differ when only "endless scruples and errors of dangerous consequence arise in morality" and "philosophers retire into themselves and attentively consider their own meaning" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: the conclusion is read berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1 with no modality; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: the text does not say which it means
berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1 puts it at S3, "Hence arise endless scruples and errors of dangerous consequence in morality."; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3 at S4, "All which, I doubt not, may be cleared, and truth appear plain, uniform, and consistent, could but philosophers be prevailed on to retire into themselves, and attentively consider their own meaning."
berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r1.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S3, "Hence arise endless scruples and errors of dangerous consequence in morality.", rather than S4, "All which, I doubt not, may be cleared, and truth appear plain, uniform, and consistent, could but philosophers be prevailed on to retire into themselves, and attentively consider their own meaning."?
